## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design a modular, auditable routing layer for prompts with data-sensitivity and reproducibility concerns. It emphasizes concrete signals, edge-case handling, and a small prototype to demonstrate flow control.

## Key Concepts

- Prompt routing signals: intent, data_sensitivity, reproducibility
- Edge-case handling: ambiguity, missing sources, conflicting data
- Lightweight prototyping: minimal module map and payload schema

## Code Example

```javascript
function routePrompt(prompt) {
  const text = prompt.toLowerCase()
  const toLiterature = /(summarize|literature|research)/.test(text)
  const toExperiment = /(experiment|plan|propose)/.test(text)
  const needsAudit = /(audit|trace|reproducible)/.test(text)

  if (toLiterature && toExperiment && needsAudit) {
    return {
      modules: ['Literature-Summary', 'Experiment-Plan', 'Audit-Log-Formatter'],
      payload: { prompt, sources: [], trace: { confidence: 0.8 } }
    }
  }
  // Additional routing logic...
}
```

## Why This Is Asked
Tests practical prompt versioning and drift handling with concrete routing decisions for enterprise data assistants.

## Key Concepts
- Drift detection via keyword overlap scoring
- Baseline prompt management with patch generation
- Three-tier routing: Intent-Detector, Safety-Guard, Response-Composer
- Signal tracking: intent, data_sensitivity, version_delta
- Edge case handling: renamed intents, missing baselines

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo-code for drift detection and routing
function routePrompt(newPrompt, baselinePrompt) {
  const overlap = calculateKeywordOverlap(newPrompt, baselinePrompt);
  const hasSafetyIssues = detectSafetyKeywords(newPrompt);

  if (hasSafetyIssues) return 'Safety-Guard';
  if (overlap < 0.6) return 'Intent-Detector';
  return 'Response-Composer';
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you calibrate the overlap threshold for different use cases?
- What approach would you use to store patches for audit trails?
- How would you handle version conflicts when multiple teams modify prompts simultaneously?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests ability to design per-tenant policy enforcement, guard-detection, and prompt rewriting with auditable provenance.

## Key Concepts

- Multi-tenant policy registry and per-tenant constraints
- Guard-detection vs. prompt rewriting
- System guard injection and explicit privacy constraints
- Audit artifacts and drift/telemetry considerations

## Code Example

```javascript
// Minimal prototype sketch
const policy = {
  Tesla: { pii: true, retentionDays: 90 },
  Google: { pii: false, retentionDays: 365 },
  Plaid: { pii: true, retentionDays: 30 }
};
function guardAndRewrite(tenant, prompt) {
  const violations = [];
  const lower = prompt.toLowerCase();
  if (lower.includes("password") || lower.includes("ssn")) {
    violations.push("PII-like content");
  }
  const rewritten = `[${tenant}] ${prompt} /* guard: ensure policy compliance */`;
  return { rewritten, violations };
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you evaluate determinism and drift for two tenants under evolving policies?  
- What tests would you add to catch policy-evading prompts without compromising latency?

